CAT = "hs2Risk"; SEG = "RSK"
Q = [
("What is a hazard?",
 "Anything with the potential to cause harm", ["The chance that harm will happen", "An injury that needs first aid", "A report made after an accident"],
 "A hazard is something that can cause harm, such as a chemical, electricity or a wet floor. Risk is the chance it will cause harm, and how badly."),
("What is risk?",
 "Likelihood of harm and how severe it is", ["Any object that can hurt you", "A rule written by the employer", "The cost of a safety control"],
 "Risk combines how likely it is that a hazard will cause harm with how serious that harm could be."),
("A trailing cable runs across a corridor. In risk assessment terms the cable is:",
 "The hazard", ["The risk", "The control", "The outcome"],
 "The cable can cause harm, so it is the hazard. The chance of someone tripping over it and how badly they are hurt is the risk."),
("What is the first step in HSE's approach to risk assessment?",
 "Identify the hazards", ["Record the findings", "Decide on precautions", "Review the assessment"],
 "HSE's steps run: identify hazards, decide who might be harmed and how, evaluate the risks and decide on precautions, record findings, then review."),
("After identifying hazards, what is the next step of a risk assessment?",
 "Decide who might be harmed and how", ["Buy personal protective equipment", "Write the safety policy", "Report to the HSE"],
 "Step two is to think about who could be harmed, such as staff, cleaners, visitors or the public, and how."),
("Which law requires employers to assess the risks to employees and others?",
 "Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999", ["Workplace Regulations 1992", "RIDDOR 2013", "Safety Signs Regulations 1996"],
 "Regulation 3 of the Management Regulations requires a suitable and sufficient assessment of risks to employees and to others affected by the work."),
("From how many employees must an employer record the significant findings of its risk assessment?",
 "5 or more", ["1 or more", "10 or more", "50 or more"],
 "An employer with five or more employees must record the significant findings and any group identified as especially at risk."),
("What makes a risk assessment 'suitable and sufficient'?",
 "It finds the significant risks", ["It lists every possible hazard", "It runs to many pages", "A consultant signs it"],
 "It must identify the significant risks and the controls needed, in proportion to the work. It does not have to cover trivial risks."),
("Which group should a risk assessment pay particular attention to?",
 "New and expectant mothers", ["Staff with long service", "Managers and supervisors", "Workers on day shifts"],
 "The Management Regulations need specific assessment of risks to new and expectant mothers, such as heavy lifting and some chemicals."),
("Why do workers under 18 need particular attention in a risk assessment?",
 "Inexperience and unawareness of risk", ["They are legally banned from work", "They cannot sign the assessment", "They never need any training"],
 "Regulation 19 recognises that young people may lack experience, awareness of risk and maturity, so their risks must be assessed before they start."),
("When should a risk assessment be reviewed?",
 "When something significant changes", ["Only when the HSE asks", "Only if a court orders it", "Once, when the firm opens"],
 "Review it after changes to equipment, processes or staff, after accidents or near misses, and regularly so it stays up to date."),
("An accident shows a control has failed. What should happen to the risk assessment?",
 "Review it and update the controls", ["Leave it, as it was signed off", "Throw it away and start a log", "Send it to the insurer only"],
 "Accidents and near misses are triggers for review, since they show the assessment or its controls are not working."),
("A risk scores 'likely' for chance and 'major injury' for severity. What does that mean for action?",
 "It is high and needs urgent control", ["It can be accepted as it is", "It only needs recording", "It is low and can wait a year"],
 "Many assessments multiply likelihood by severity. A likely, serious outcome gives a high rating that calls for prompt action."),
("What is residual risk?",
 "Risk left after controls are in place", ["Risk from waste materials", "Risk to people outside the site", "Risk that has been removed"],
 "Even after controls, some risk usually remains. The assessment should show this is as low as reasonably practicable."),
("Who must follow the controls that a risk assessment sets out?",
 "Every employee it applies to", ["Only the manager who wrote it", "Only staff who agree with it", "Only the safety representative"],
 "Employees must co-operate with the employer and follow safe systems of work and controls identified by risk assessment."),
("Why should the people doing a task be involved in assessing it?",
 "They know how the job is really done", ["So the employer can blame them", "So no manager has to sign it", "To avoid having to record it"],
 "Workers can point out hazards and shortcuts a manager might miss, which makes the assessment more accurate."),
("Who might be harmed by floor cleaning in a supermarket during opening hours?",
 "Customers as well as staff", ["Only the cleaner", "Only the store manager", "Only delivery drivers"],
 "Members of the public are affected by work activity, so they must be considered as well as employees."),
("Which source helps identify less obvious hazards?",
 "Past accident and ill-health records", ["The firm's sales figures", "Staff holiday rotas", "The building's floor plan only"],
 "HSE advises looking back at accident and ill-health records, as well as manufacturers' instructions and data sheets."),
("Which assessment must be done before staff use a hazardous cleaning chemical?",
 "COSHH assessment", ["Display screen assessment", "Manual handling assessment", "Noise assessment"],
 "The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations require the risks from hazardous substances to be assessed and controlled."),
("What is a safe system of work?",
 "A set way of doing a task safely", ["A list of staff phone numbers", "A computer firewall", "An insurance policy"],
 "A safe system of work is a planned procedure that removes hazards or controls the risks for a task, often written down for higher-risk work."),
("What is a permit to work used for?",
 "Controlling high-risk tasks like confined spaces", ["Allowing overtime at weekends", "Letting visitors park on site", "Approving staff holidays"],
 "A permit to work is a formal written system that controls high-risk work, such as entry into confined spaces or hot work."),
("Why must a risk assessment be shared with the employees it covers?",
 "They need to know the risks and controls", ["It is a secret company document", "It is only for the insurer", "It is only for inspectors to see"],
 "Employers must give employees understandable information on the risks identified and the controls in place."),
("What is the main purpose of risk assessment?",
 "To prevent harm by deciding on controls", ["To produce paperwork for inspectors", "To decide who to blame for accidents", "To set staff wages"],
 "Risk assessment is about identifying sensible measures to control risks, not about creating paperwork."),
("A worker tells their manager they are pregnant. What must the employer do?",
 "Check the risks to her and her baby", ["Move her to part-time work", "Ask her to leave the job", "Wait until she returns from leave"],
 "Once told in writing, the employer must review the risks to the worker and act to remove or reduce them, for example by changing duties."),
("What does 'severity' measure in a risk rating?",
 "How bad the harm could be", ["How often the task is done", "How many hazards there are", "How old the equipment is"],
 "Severity describes how serious the outcome could be, from minor injury to death, while likelihood describes how probable it is."),
("An assessment lists a hazard but no controls. What has the assessor missed?",
 "Deciding on precautions", ["Identifying the hazard", "Naming the workplace", "Dating the document"],
 "Evaluating the risk and deciding what precautions are needed is a core step; without it the assessment changes nothing."),
("Which is an example of a near miss?",
 "A box falls but hits nobody", ["A worker needs stitches", "A fire damages a store room", "A van hits a parked car"],
 "A near miss is an event that could have caused harm but did not. Recording them helps prevent future accidents."),
("A lone worker cleans offices at night. Which extra control does their risk assessment most likely need?",
 "A way to raise the alarm", ["A second safety policy", "A taller step ladder", "A daytime fire drill"],
 "People working alone need a way to summon help, such as a check-in procedure or personal alarm, if something goes wrong."),
]
